{"caseId":"OD2-HO-OBVWRONG-2","methodChoice":"EDF-2 analysis using initial evidence plus X11 (one catalogue item collected; stopped after it because it settled the main discriminating question).","answers":[{"itemId":"I1","answer":"unknown","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["E1","E4","X11"]},{"itemId":"I2","answer":"supported","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["X11","E5","E1"]},{"itemId":"I3","answer":"unknown","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["E2","E4","X11"]},{"itemId":"I4","answer":"contradicted","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["X11"]},{"itemId":"I5","answer":"unknown","confidence":55,"evidenceIds":["X11","E5"]},{"itemId":"I6","answer":"contradicted","confidence":50,"evidenceIds":["X11","E1","E3"]},{"itemId":"I7","answer":"causal","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["X11","E5","E1"]},{"itemId":"I8","answer":"causal","confidence":65,"evidenceIds":["E4","X11","E1"]},{"itemId":"I9","answer":"association-only","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["E5","X11"]},{"itemId":"I10","answer":"harmful","confidence":65,"evidenceIds":["X11","E6","E4"]},{"itemId":"I11","answer":"preferred","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["X11","E5"]},{"itemId":"I12","answer":"premature","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["X11"]},{"itemId":"I13","answer":"acceptable","confidence":55,"evidenceIds":["E4","X11"]},{"itemId":"I14","answer":"sufficient","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["X11","E5"]}],"brief":"What is going on: the rise is mostly a bag-tag problem, not a handling problem. Since 1 September, Corvane tags printed on the new Fennick Labels stock fail automatic reading about 11.8% of the time, against 1.5% for the old Brackwater stock. Counter-printed and kiosk-printed tags fail at similar rates. When counters briefly used Brackwater stock on 14–16 October, their failure rate fell to 1.6%, while kiosk tags issued the same days still failed at 12%. Other carriers' tags read normally on the same scanners, so the scanners are not the problem. Diverted bags then miss the 35-minute lateral closure.

How sure: moderately. The timing and the switch-back result are strong. Whether the fault is the stock itself or the printer heat setting is not settled, and I did not test the handler-crew or evening-bank explanations directly.

Next: switch counters and kiosks back to Brackwater stock now; it is valid to 31 December and about five weeks is in store. Test Fennick stock separately before any defect claim. Hold the breach notice: the data does not show Tessaly caused this, and the notice would penalise the wrong party. Consider a temporary evening encode workstation while the switch-back takes effect."}
